1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,792
A china rose in the college garden.

2
00:00:02,792 --> 00:00:04,496
A pea flower on a vine.

3
00:00:04,496 --> 00:00:06,994
A small white guava flower on a tree.

4
00:00:07,394 --> 00:00:09,468
Three flowers, three secrets.

5
00:00:09,468 --> 00:00:11,665
One splits evenly in many ways.

6
00:00:11,665 --> 00:00:13,241
One in just one way.

7
00:00:13,241 --> 00:00:16,322
And one hides its ovary
below everything else.

8
00:00:16,772 --> 00:00:20,449
Diya asked: how do botanists describe
a flower so exactly that anyone can

9
00:00:20,449 --> 00:00:21,349
picture it?

10
00:00:21,537 --> 00:00:23,020
They ask three questions.

11
00:00:23,020 --> 00:00:24,996
What rings of parts does it have?

12
00:00:24,996 --> 00:00:26,138
How is it shaped?

13
00:00:26,138 --> 00:00:27,893
And where does the ovary sit?

14
00:00:28,393 --> 00:00:31,331
By the end, you will read any
flower with these three questions.

15
00:00:31,981 --> 00:00:33,275
First, the parts.

16
00:00:33,275 --> 00:00:36,080
A flower sits on a stalk
called the pedicel.

17
00:00:36,080 --> 00:00:39,785
Its swollen top is the thalamus,
also called the receptacle.

18
00:00:40,235 --> 00:00:42,513
On the thalamus grow four whorls.

19
00:00:42,513 --> 00:00:46,263
A whorl is a ring of parts,
one ring inside the next.

20
00:00:46,863 --> 00:00:50,711
The calyx is made of sepals, the
outermost ring, usually green.

21
00:00:50,711 --> 00:00:54,605
The corolla is made of petals,
often bright, to attract insects.

22
00:00:55,205 --> 00:00:58,022
The androecium is made of
stamens, the male parts.

23
00:00:58,022 --> 00:01:01,961
The gynoecium is made of carpels, the
female parts, with the ovary at the

24
00:01:01,961 --> 00:01:02,861
bottom.

25
00:01:03,109 --> 00:01:06,794
In some flowers, like lily, the
sepals and petals look alike.

26
00:01:06,794 --> 00:01:09,017
Together they are called the perianth.

27
00:01:09,667 --> 00:01:12,545
A flower with both stamens
and carpels is bisexual.

28
00:01:12,545 --> 00:01:15,123
A flower with only one
of them is unisexual.

29
00:01:15,773 --> 00:01:17,176
Question two: symmetry.

30
00:01:17,176 --> 00:01:19,573
Imagine cutting a flower
through its centre.

31
00:01:19,923 --> 00:01:24,846
If every cut through the centre gives two
equal halves, the flower is actinomorphic,

32
00:01:24,846 --> 00:01:26,838
which means radially symmetrical.

33
00:01:26,838 --> 00:01:29,360
Mustard, datura and chilli are examples.

34
00:01:30,110 --> 00:01:34,269
If only one cut gives two similar
halves, the flower is zygomorphic, which

35
00:01:34,269 --> 00:01:35,966
means bilaterally symmetrical.

36
00:01:35,966 --> 00:01:38,666
Pea, gulmohur, bean and
Cassia are examples.

37
00:01:39,416 --> 00:01:44,925
And if no cut gives two similar halves,
the flower is asymmetric, or irregular,

38
00:01:44,925 --> 00:01:45,910
like canna.

39
00:01:46,660 --> 00:01:48,650
Flowers also count their parts.

40
00:01:48,650 --> 00:01:53,462
With parts in multiples of three, four
or five, they are trimerous, tetramerous

41
00:01:53,462 --> 00:01:54,523
or pentamerous.

42
00:01:55,173 --> 00:01:58,453
A small reduced leaf at the
base of the pedicel is a bract.

43
00:01:58,453 --> 00:02:00,308
Flowers with bracts are bracteate.

44
00:02:00,308 --> 00:02:01,775
Without them, ebracteate.

45
00:02:02,425 --> 00:02:04,981
Question three: where does the ovary sit?

46
00:02:04,981 --> 00:02:06,231
Watch the thalamus.

47
00:02:06,581 --> 00:02:07,481
Hypogynous.

48
00:02:07,380 --> 00:02:11,491
The gynoecium sits at the highest
point, and the other parts are below

49
00:02:11,491 --> 00:02:12,391
it.

50
00:02:11,918 --> 00:02:15,780
The ovary is superior, as in
mustard, china rose and brinjal.

51
00:02:16,530 --> 00:02:17,430
Perigynous.

52
00:02:17,371 --> 00:02:19,132
The thalamus forms a cup.

53
00:02:19,132 --> 00:02:23,683
The gynoecium is in the centre, and
the other parts sit on the rim, at

54
00:02:23,683 --> 00:02:25,238
almost the same level.

55
00:02:25,238 --> 00:02:29,015
The ovary is half inferior,
as in plum, rose and peach.

56
00:02:29,765 --> 00:02:30,665
Epigynous.

57
00:02:30,583 --> 00:02:35,207
The rim of the thalamus grows up,
wraps the ovary completely, and fuses

58
00:02:35,207 --> 00:02:36,107
with it.

59
00:02:35,984 --> 00:02:38,578
The other parts arise above the ovary.

60
00:02:38,578 --> 00:02:43,969
The ovary is inferior, as in guava,
cucumber, and the ray florets of sunflower.

61
00:02:44,719 --> 00:02:47,938
Now the two ends of this
scale, side by side.

62
00:02:48,288 --> 00:02:52,493
Both flowers can have all four whorls,
and both keep their ovules inside

63
00:02:52,493 --> 00:02:53,393
the ovary.

64
00:02:53,751 --> 00:02:58,592
But in a hypogynous flower, the
ovary is on top, and it is superior.

65
00:02:58,592 --> 00:03:04,062
In an epigynous flower, the ovary is
below the other parts, and it is inferior.

66
00:03:04,712 --> 00:03:05,612
Your turn.

67
00:03:05,533 --> 00:03:09,423
In a guava flower, the sepals
and petals sit above the ovary.

68
00:03:09,423 --> 00:03:11,746
Is the ovary superior, or inferior?

69
00:03:11,746 --> 00:03:12,955
Pause and decide.

70
00:03:16,155 --> 00:03:17,055
Inferior!

71
00:03:16,894 --> 00:03:21,097
The thalamus wraps the ovary, and
the other parts arise above it.

72
00:03:21,097 --> 00:03:22,644
So guava is epigynous.

73
00:03:23,294 --> 00:03:24,457
Remember this.

74
00:03:24,457 --> 00:03:26,499
Superior ovary: hypogynous.

75
00:03:26,499 --> 00:03:28,488
Half inferior: perigynous.

76
00:03:28,488 --> 00:03:30,476
Inferior ovary: epigynous.

77
00:03:31,076 --> 00:03:32,733
Now, a worked example.

78
00:03:32,733 --> 00:03:34,342
Let's read four flowers.

79
00:03:34,692 --> 00:03:38,375
Mustard: every cut through
the centre gives equal halves.

80
00:03:38,375 --> 00:03:39,881
That is actinomorphic.

81
00:03:40,331 --> 00:03:43,075
Pea: only one cut gives similar halves.

82
00:03:43,075 --> 00:03:44,517
That is zygomorphic.

83
00:03:44,967 --> 00:03:48,944
Rose: the other parts sit on the
rim of a cup, around the ovary.

84
00:03:48,944 --> 00:03:50,167
That is perigynous.

85
00:03:50,617 --> 00:03:54,309
Cucumber: the ovary sits
below all the other parts.

86
00:03:54,309 --> 00:03:55,737
That is epigynous.

87
00:03:56,387 --> 00:03:57,848
Now, the exam trap.

88
00:03:58,198 --> 00:04:02,454
Many students think hypogynous means
the ovary is low, because hypo means

89
00:04:02,454 --> 00:04:03,354
below.

90
00:04:03,428 --> 00:04:07,092
But it is the other parts
that sit below the gynoecium.

91
00:04:07,092 --> 00:04:10,394
So in a hypogynous flower,
the ovary is on top.

92
00:04:10,394 --> 00:04:11,576
It is superior.

93
00:04:12,226 --> 00:04:13,749
Where is this asked?

94
00:04:13,749 --> 00:04:18,382
As matching a flower with its symmetry
or ovary position, or as labelled

95
00:04:18,382 --> 00:04:19,282
diagrams.

96
00:04:19,759 --> 00:04:20,908
Exam level now.

97
00:04:20,908 --> 00:04:23,060
Match each term with its flower.

98
00:04:23,060 --> 00:04:24,258
Pause and solve.

99
00:04:29,658 --> 00:04:31,062
The answer is A.

100
00:04:31,062 --> 00:04:33,817
A three, B one, C four, D two.

101
00:04:34,317 --> 00:04:37,397
Pea is zygomorphic, and
canna is asymmetric.

102
00:04:37,897 --> 00:04:42,611
Plum is perigynous, with a half
inferior ovary, and guava is epigynous,

103
00:04:42,611 --> 00:04:44,284
with an inferior ovary.

104
00:04:44,884 --> 00:04:46,424
Here is your recap.

105
00:04:46,424 --> 00:04:48,007
Screenshot this card.

106
00:04:51,207 --> 00:04:53,393
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

107
00:04:53,793 --> 00:04:54,693
One.

108
00:04:54,211 --> 00:04:55,392
What is a perianth?

109
00:04:56,292 --> 00:04:57,192
Two.

110
00:04:56,769 --> 00:04:58,538
Name one zygomorphic flower.

111
00:04:59,438 --> 00:05:00,338
Three.

112
00:04:59,956 --> 00:05:02,847
In which type of flower is
the ovary half inferior?

113
00:05:03,947 --> 00:05:08,789
Next time: open a flower bud, and the
petals are folded in a hidden pattern.

114
00:05:08,789 --> 00:05:10,190
How are they packed?

115
00:05:10,190 --> 00:05:12,889
We find out in aestivation
and placentation.
